mt-5"><div class=3D"overflow-hidden [&amp;&gt;*:first-child]:mt-0 [&amp;&gt=
;*:last-child]:mb-0"><p>A production application rarely stores its knowledg=
e in a single folder of text files. Your company data lives across remote s=
ervices: customer tickets in Zendesk, engineering specifications in GitHub,=
 team updates in Slack, and meeting notes in Google Docs. To feed this data=
 into a language model, you need a way to connect to these external APIs, a=
uthenticate safely, and turn their responses into standardized LlamaIndex <=
button type=3D"button" class=3D"cursor-pointer rounded-sm bg-yellow-100 px-=
0.5 font-normal text-inherit underline decoration-yellow-500 decoration-dot=
ted underline-offset-3 hover:bg-yellow-200">Document</button> objects.</p>
<p>LlamaHub is the central registry of community-maintained and official da=
ta loaders for LlamaIndex. Instead of writing custom API integration code f=
or every SaaS tool, you install a specialized reader that handles paginatio=
n, API rate limits, and response conversion for you.</p>
<h2>How Readers Work</h2>
<p>In the previous lesson, you used <code>SimpleDirectoryReader</code> to t=
urn local files into <code>Document</code> objects. Remote readers work on =
the exact same interface contract.</p>
<p>Every remote reader inherits from <code>BaseReader</code>. A reader take=
s external configuration during initialization (such as API tokens or base =
URLs), contacts the third-party service, and returns a Python list of <code=
>Document</code> objects through its <code>load_data()</code> method.</p>
