<p>The <code>SimpleWebPageReader</code> makes standard HTTP <code>GET</code=
> requests, parses HTML payloads into plain text using an HTML-to-text pars=
er, and assigns the source URL to the document.</p>
<p>However, real-world remote APIs present three problems that local files =
do not:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong><button type=3D"button" class=3D"cursor-pointer rounded-sm bg-y=
ellow-100 px-0.5 font-normal text-inherit underline decoration-yellow-500 d=
ecoration-dotted underline-offset-3 hover:bg-yellow-200">Rate limits</butto=
n></strong>: External APIs reject requests if you ask for too many items pe=
r minute.</li>
<li><strong><button type=3D"button" class=3D"cursor-pointer rounded-sm bg-y=
ellow-100 px-0.5 font-normal text-inherit underline decoration-yellow-500 d=
ecoration-dotted underline-offset-3 hover:bg-yellow-200">Pagination</button=
></strong>: Large datasets split across hundreds of API pages.</li>
<li><strong>Transient network errors</strong>: HTTP connections drop, time =
out, or return <code>503 Service Unavailable</code>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Production readers on LlamaHub handle basic pagination automatically. Bu=
t you must configure your application to manage rate limits and network ret=
ries.</p>
