<h2>Exercises</h2>
<p>Complete these exercises to practice using remote readers:</p>
<ol>
<li>Install <code>llama-index-readers-web</code>. Write a script that loads=
 the text from three public technical blog posts into a single list of <cod=
e>Document</code> objects using <code>SimpleWebPageReader</code>. Print the=
 length of the text and the source URL for each document.</li>
<li>Modify your script to print the keys present in the <code>metadata</cod=
e> dictionary of the loaded web documents. Verify which fields are automati=
cally generated by the web reader.</li>
<li>Write a custom reader subclassing <code>BaseReader</code> that reads it=
ems from a local JSON file containing customer reviews (each with an <code>=
id</code>, <code>rating</code>, <code>title</code>, and <code>review_text</=
code>), and outputs a <code>Document</code> for each review with appropriat=
e metadata fields.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>LlamaHub readers provide standard interfaces to ingest data from third-p=
arty APIs, SaaS platforms, and internal services into LlamaIndex. Every rea=
der produces standard <code>Document</code> objects containing raw text and=
 metadata dictionaries. Using dedicated packages like <code>llama-index-rea=
ders-github</code> and methods like <code>lazy_load_data()</code> ensures y=
our data ingestion remains secure, predictable, and memory-efficient.</p>
<p>Next, you will learn how to enrich, clean, and configure the metadata in=
side these <code>Document</code> objects to prepare them for precise downst=
ream retrieval.</p></div></div><div class=3D"mt-12 flex flex-col gap-4 max-=
